==English==
[[Image:A_full_moon_reflecting_off_the_river_-_NOAA.jpg|thumb|Moonlight]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|monelight}}, {{m|enm|monelicht}}, equivalent to {{af|en|moon|light}}. Compare {{cog|sco|munelicht}}, {{m|sco|muinlicht}}, {{cog|fy|moanneljocht}}, {{cog|nl|maanlicht}}, {{cog|de|Mondlicht}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈmunˌlaɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-moonlight.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|moon|light|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|sometimes|attributive}} The [[light#Noun|light]] [[reflect]]ed from the [[Moon]].
#* c. '''1387''', {{w|Geoffrey Chaucer}}, [[w:Sir Thopas|The Tale of Sir Thopas]] in ''{{w|The Canterbury Tales}}'',<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2383/pg2383-images.html]</sup>
#*: His bridle as the sunne shone,
#*: Or as the '''moonelight'''.
#* c. '''1595''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|A Midsummer Night’s Dream}}'', Act II, Scene 1<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=midsummer&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: If you will patiently dance in our round
#*: And see our '''moonlight''' revels, go with us;
#*: If not, shun me, and I will spare your haunts.
#* c. '''1596''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|The Merchant of Venice}}'', Act V, Scene 1,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=merchantvenice&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: How sweet the '''moonlight''' sleeps upon this bank!
#*: Here will we sit and let the sounds of music
#*: Creep in our ears: soft stillness and the night
#*: Become the touches of sweet harmony.
#* '''1751''', {{w|Tobias Smollett}}, ''{{w|The Adventures of Peregrine Pickle}}'', Chapter 24,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/4084/4084-h/4084-h.htm]</sup>
#*: {{...}} the sight of the blade which glistened by '''moonlight''' in his face, checked, in some sort, the ardour of his assailant {{...}}
#* '''1798''', {{w|William Wordworth}}, “The Idiot Boy,” lines 1-4,<sup>[http://www.bartleby.com/145/ww137.html]</sup>
#*: ’Tis eight o’clock,—a clear March night,
#*: The moon is up,—the sky is blue,
#*: The owlet, in the '''moonlight''' air,
#*: Shouts from nobody knows where;
#* '''1830''', {{w|Oliver Wendell Holmes}}, “Ballad of the Oysterman,” lines 5-6,<sup>[http://www.bartleby.com/380/poem/43.html]</sup>
#*: It was the pensive oysterman that saw a lovely maid,
#*: Upon a '''moonlight''' evening, a-sitting in the shade;
#* '''1849''', {{w|Charlotte Brontë}}, ''[[w:Shirley (novel)|Shirley]]'', Chapter 13,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/30486/30486-h/30486-h.htm]</sup>
#*: She passed away noiselessly, and the '''moonlight''' kissed the wall which her shadow had dimmed.
#* '''1889''', {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, ''{{w|The Master of Ballantrae}}'', Chapter 12,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/864/864-h/864-h.htm]</sup>
#*: “{{...}} What say you, gentlemen, shall we have a '''moonlight''' hunt?”
#* '''1897''', {{w|Bram Stoker}}, ''{{w|Dracula}}'', Chapter 3,[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/45839/45839-h/45839-h.htm]
#*: The windows were curtainless, and the yellow '''moonlight''', flooding in through the diamond panes, enabled one to see even colours, whilst it softened the wealth of dust which lay over all and disguised in some measure the ravages of time and the moth.
#* '''1925''', {{w|F. Scott Fitzgerald}}, ''{{w|The Great Gatsby}}'', Chapter 6,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=uRVGCgAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: They were still under the white plum tree and their faces were touching except for a pale thin ray of '''moonlight''' between.
#* '''1937''', {{w|J. R. R. Tolkien}}, ''{{w|The Hobbit}}'', Del Rey, 1982, Chapter 16, p. 272,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=hFfhrCWiLSMC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: It was as if a globe had been filled with '''moonlight''' and hung before them in a net woven of the glint of frosty stars.
#* '''1957''', {{w|Sylvia Dee}}, “{{w|Moonlight Swim}}” (song recorded by {{w|Nick Noble}} and {{w|Elvis Presley}}),<sup>[http://www.azlyrics.com/lyrics/elvispresley/moonlightswim.html]</sup>
#*: Let’s go on a '''moonlight''' swim
#*: Far away from the crowd
#*: All alone upon the beach
#*: Our lips and our arms
#*: Close within each other’s reach
#*: Will be on a '''moonlight''' swim
#* '''1958''', {{w|Chinua Achebe}}, ''{{w|Things Fall Apart}}'', London: William Heinemann, Chapter 2,
#*: On a '''moonlight''' night it would be different. The happy voices of children playing in open fields would then be heard. And perhaps those not so young would be playing in pairs in less open places, and old men and women would remember their youth.

